.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt GEN_TRAFFIC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gen_traffic
.Nd write a synthetic pcap file of background and DoS/DDoS traffic
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -out Ar file
.Op Fl -seed Ar n
.Op Fl -background Ar sec
.Op Fl -events Ar n
.Op Fl -pattern Ar name
.Op Fl -target Ar ip Ns Op : Ns Ar port
.Op Fl -start Ar sec
.Op Fl -duration Ar sec
.Op Fl -rate Ar n
.Op Fl -bots Ar n
.Op Fl -spoof
.Op Fl -payload Ar n
.Nm
.Fl -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
writes one pcap file of synthetic traffic: a background mix of
ordinary events
.Pq web visits, DNS lookups, pings and internal file-share visits ,
plus, if
.Fl -pattern
is given, one attack on top of it.
See
.Sx BACKGROUND TRAFFIC
below for the mix.
It opens no socket and touches no network interface; every Ethernet,
IPv4, TCP, UDP and ICMP header is built by hand into a memory buffer
and written straight to the file.
The only output is that file and, alongside it, a label file.
.Pp
Every address
.Nm
uses is either in the lab's own private range
.Pq Li 10.1.0.0/24
or in a range RFC 5737 reserves for documentation
.Pq Li 192.0.2.0/24 , Li 198.51.100.0/24 , Li 203.0.113.0/24 ,
none of which is ever routed on the real Internet.
The one fake domain name it generates ends in
.Li .example ,
reserved the same way by RFC 2606.
Running
.Nm
can only ever produce a file.
.Pp
Given the same
.Fl -seed ,
.Nm
always writes the same file, which is what keeps a label file correct
across runs.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-background sec"
.It Fl -out Ar file
Output pcap file.
Required.
.It Fl -seed Ar n
PRNG seed.
Default 1.
.It Fl -background Ar sec
Length of the capture, in seconds.
Default 120.
.It Fl -events Ar n
Number of background events spread across the capture; see
.Sx BACKGROUND TRAFFIC
for the mix.
Default 40.
.It Fl -pattern Ar name
Attack to generate on top of the background traffic.
See
.Sx PATTERNS
below.
Omit it for a clean, attack-free file.
.It Fl -target Ar ip Ns Op : Ns Ar port
Attack target, or the victim for
.Ar reflection .
Default
.Li 10.1.0.5 ,
with a pattern-specific default port if none is given.
.It Fl -start Ar sec
When the attack begins, in seconds from the start of the capture.
Default is half of
.Fl -background .
.It Fl -duration Ar sec
How long the attack lasts, in seconds.
Default 30.
.It Fl -rate Ar n
Attack units per second, for every pattern except
.Ar beacon ,
where it is instead the interval between beacons, in seconds.
Default 10.
.It Fl -bots Ar n
Number of distinct source hosts.
Default 1, except
.Ar reflection
(20) and
.Ar httpflood
(30), which default to many sources unless
.Fl -bots
is given explicitly.
.It Fl -spoof
For
.Ar synflood
and
.Ar udpflood :
draw a fresh random source address for every packet, instead of a
fixed pool of
.Fl -bots
addresses.
.It Fl -payload Ar n
Payload size in bytes, for
.Ar udpflood
(default 60) and
.Ar reflection
(default 500).
.It Fl -help
Print a short usage summary with defaults and worked examples, then
exit.
.El
.Sh PATTERNS
.Bl -tag -width "icmpflood"
.It Ar synflood
TCP SYNs to one target port, never answered.
Single source by default; distributed with
.Fl -bots
and
.Fl -spoof .
.It Ar udpflood
UDP datagrams of random payload to one host.
Single source by default; distributed with
.Fl -bots
and
.Fl -spoof .
.It Ar icmpflood
ICMP echo requests at a fixed rate.
.It Ar dns
Queries for a random 8-character hex label under the fixed
.Li .example
domain, the random-subdomain
.Pq Dq water torture
pattern.
Single source by default; distributed with
.Fl -bots .
.It Ar reflection
Large UDP replies from source port 123
.Pq NTP ,
from many reflectors, to one victim: the view from the victim's
vantage point, since the spoofed request that triggered each reply
was never addressed there.
Distributed by default.
.It Ar beacon
One short, complete TCP connection every
.Fl -rate
seconds, with jitter, from one fixed source: a command-and-control
beacon.
.It Ar httpflood
Many short, complete TCP connections from many hosts to one port.
Distributed by default.
.El
.Sh BACKGROUND TRAFFIC
Each of the
.Fl -events
background events is one lab client
.Pq Li 10.1.0.20 Ns - Ns Li 59
picked at random, doing one of the following, chosen by weighted
roll:
.Bl -tag -width "55%"
.It 55%
A web visit: a complete TCP exchange to
.Li 10.1.0.5:443 .
.It 25%
A DNS lookup: one query for a random label under
.Li .example
to the resolver,
.Li 10.1.0.1:53 .
.It 12%
A ping: an ICMP echo request to
.Li 10.1.0.1
and its reply.
.It 8%
An internal file-share visit: a complete TCP exchange to
.Li 10.1.0.6:445 .
.El
.Pp
The web visits land on
.Li 10.1.0.5:443 ,
the same host and port
.Ar synflood
and
.Ar beacon
use by default: a legitimate baseline on that service for those two
attacks to stand out against, the way a real flood or a real C2
beacon would.
The DNS lookups use the same port
.Pq 53
as the
.Ar dns
pattern's default target port, but a different host
.Pq the resolver, not Li 10.1.0.5 .
Every other pattern and every other background event kind use
disjoint hosts, ports or protocols.
.Sh OUTPUT
.Fl -out Ar file
writes the pcap, and
.Pa file Ns Li .labels.csv
alongside it, with one header row and at most one data row in
.Li rule,src,start,end
format.
.Pp
For a single clear source
.Pq one bot, no Fl -spoof ,
.Li src
names the attacker.
For a distributed pattern it names the
.Em victim
instead, since a per-source rule has no single attacker to key a
label on; detecting that traffic needs a per-target count.
A file with no
.Fl -pattern
gets a label file with only the header row.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A bad option, a missing
.Fl -out ,
an unknown
.Fl -pattern ,
or a malformed address terminates
.Nm
immediately with status 1.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Background traffic only, to check that a detector raises nothing on
quiet traffic:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./gen_traffic --out clean.pcap --background 120
.Ed
.Pp
A SYN flood from one source, starting 60 seconds in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./gen_traffic --out synflood.pcap --background 120 \e
    --pattern synflood --start 60 --duration 20 --rate 30
.Ed
.Pp
The same flood, spread over 20 spoofed sources instead of one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./gen_traffic --out synflood_dist.pcap --background 120 \e
    --pattern synflood --start 60 --duration 20 --rate 30 \e
    --bots 20 --spoof
.Ed
.Pp
A beacon roughly every 20 seconds;
.Fl -duration
must cover several periods:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./gen_traffic --out beacon.pcap --background 300 \e
    --pattern beacon --rate 20 --duration 300
.Ed
.Sh CREATING A DATASET
.Nm
has no single flag for dataset size or class balance.
Each run writes one labeled file; a dataset is many such runs, swept
over the parameters below and labeled by the
.Pa .labels.csv
each one writes.
.Bl -tag -width "duration/rate"
.It seed
Sweep
.Fl -seed
for distinct-but-comparable samples at fixed settings; two runs with
every other flag equal and a different seed are different traffic,
not different classes.
.It class balance
No direct ratio flag.
Background volume is
.Fl -events ;
attack volume is
.Fl -rate
times
.Fl -duration .
Balance the two sides by adjusting these against a fixed
.Fl -background
window.
.It attack type and shape
Sweep
.Fl -pattern ,
and for
.Ar synflood Ns / Ns Ar udpflood Ns / Ns Ar dns ,
.Fl -bots
and
.Fl -spoof ,
for single-source versus distributed examples of the same attack.
.It position and intensity
Sweep
.Fl -start
(early, mid, late in the window) and
.Fl -rate Ns / Ns Fl -duration
(quiet versus intense, short versus sustained) for examples that
span easy to hard to detect.
.El
.Pp
A small sweep, one file per
.Pq pattern, bots, seed
combination:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&for pat in synflood udpflood icmpflood dns reflection beacon httpflood; do
    for bots in 1 20; do
        for seed in 1 2 3 4 5; do
            ./gen_traffic --out ${pat}_b${bots}_s${seed}.pcap \e
                --seed $seed --background 120 --pattern $pat \e
                --start 60 --duration 20 --rate 30 --bots $bots
        done
    done
done
.Ed
.Pp
Add background-only files, varying only
.Fl -seed ,
for the negative class:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&for seed in 1 2 3 4 5; do
    ./gen_traffic --out clean_s${seed}.pcap --seed $seed \e
        --background 120
done
.Ed
.Pp
Ground truth for the whole set is the union of the
.Pa .labels.csv
files, not a flag
.Nm
sets directly; a clean file's label has only the header row.
